{"text": "erie\n\nFagor \u201cfo \u2018\nm\n\nApril 7,, 1972\n\nMemorandum to: Dr. Murray Senkus\n_ From: Claude M. Sawyer, Jr.\n\nSubject: Expansion Of Tobacco Using Carbon Dioxide\nand Nitrous Oxide\n\nWe have reviewed with interest the Research Department memo-\nrandum of January 14, 1972 on expanding tobacco with carbon dioxide\nand nitrous oxide. The results of the Research Department work as\ndescribed in this memorandum show that it is technically feasible\nto expand tobacco with these fluids batchwise on a very small scale\nin the laboratory, and you ask whether or not it is feasible to use\nSuch a procedure on a large, production scale.\n\nIn the laboratory work, the greatest tobacco expansion was\nobtained using pressures ranging from approximately 400 to 800 pounds\nPer square inch. From an engineering standpoint, processing tobacco\nat pressures as high as this would be difficult especially since a\nContinuous process would be preferred.\n\nAn investigation was made to determine the highest pressures\nthat are being used in continuous processing of solid materials.\nWithout disclosing what we were trying to do, we asked an expert from\nFSC Corporation what was being done in high pressure processing. He\nstated that the use of rotary valves (rotary valves are now used in\nthe G-13 process to feed and discharge the continuous pressure treater).\nhas been limited to 120 pounds per square inch. At this pressure con~\nsiderable difficulty has been experienced. There are problems with\nleakage which not only erodes the valve but also makes feeding difficult.\nProper lubrication is also difficult. He suggests that a tapered plug\nvalve with a ground fit, such as used on Pfaudler food fillers to meter\nout a can full of product, is better than a rotary valve, but it has\nsimilar problems and would not be practical in large sizes.\n\nBased on the above facts and our problems with rotary valves at\nabout 20 pounds per square inch pressure, it is concluded that such a\ncontinuous process would not be feasible in so far as present equipment\nis concerned. A program to attempt to develop new technology in this\nfield would be very expensive and the chances of success would be small.\n\n06zt 9n40s", "words": [{"text": "erie", "x0": 135, "y0": 26, "x1": 218, "y1": 39, "conf": 29.0}, {"text": "Fagor", "x0": 157, "y0": 98, "x1": 219, "y1": 115, "conf": 38.0}, {"text": "\u201cfo", "x0": 228, "y0": 89, "x1": 263, "y1": 127, "conf": 51.0}, {"text": "\u2018", "x0": 275, "y0": 105, "x1": 293, "y1": 137, "conf": 10.0}, {"text": "m", "x0": 260, "y0": 126, "x1": 276, "y1": 137, "conf": 79.0}, {"text": "April", "x0": 416, "y0": 141, "x1": 451, "y1": 155, "conf": 68.0}, {"text": "7,,", "x0": 461, "y0": 141, "x1": 474, "y1": 154, "conf": 50.0}, {"text": "1972", "x0": 484, "y0": 142, "x1": 512, "y1": 153, "conf": 50.0}, {"text": "Memorandum", "x0": 169, "y0": 197, "x1": 244, "y1": 209, "conf": 51.0}, {"text": "to:", "x0": 252, "y0": 199, "x1": 271, "y1": 210, "conf": 89.0}, {"text": "Dr.", "x0": 289, "y0": 199, "x1": 309, "y1": 211, "conf": 89.0}, {"text": "Murray", "x0": 318, "y0": 199, "x1": 363, "y1": 214, "conf": 93.0}, {"text": "Senkus", "x0": 371, "y0": 200, "x1": 414, "y1": 212, "conf": 79.0}, {"text": "_", "x0": 223, "y0": 242, "x1": 225, "y1": 244, "conf": 41.0}, {"text": "From:", "x0": 237, "y0": 228, "x1": 271, "y1": 240, "conf": 90.0}, {"text": "Claude", "x0": 288, "y0": 229, "x1": 332, "y1": 241, "conf": 86.0}, {"text": "M.", "x0": 340, "y0": 230, "x1": 353, "y1": 241, "conf": 80.0}, {"text": "Sawyer,", "x0": 363, "y0": 230, "x1": 413, "y1": 244, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "Jr.", "x0": 421, "y0": 231, "x1": 442, "y1": 242, "conf": 62.0}, {"text": "Subject:", "x0": 213, "y0": 258, "x1": 270, "y1": 272, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "Expansion", "x0": 288, "y0": 259, "x1": 354, "y1": 273, "conf": 75.0}, {"text": "Of", "x0": 362, "y0": 260, "x1": 377, "y1": 272, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "Tobacco", "x0": 384, "y0": 260, "x1": 435, "y1": 272, "c